{"kind":"report","text":"Approved. Independent implementation review of exact head c80862a0f0f4dda54d99080308a63a51209ec1e0 (branch request/mcp-linked-worktree-cache-test), against request 356d831d and candidate artifact 0971093f (path cmd/gitseq-mcp, commit c80862a0, live and non-stale, resting on live predecessor 0581839f). Reviewed in a throwaway worktree at the exact head; merge base is main 11fa38dd, so the head is one commit on current main.\n\nDIFF SCOPE: exactly one file, cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go, +50/-0, additive and test-only. No production code touched, nothing outside cmd/gitseq-mcp.\n\nMUTANT KILL (decisive, and the reason this request existed). Baseline: the new TestOneAdapterSharesRoomAcrossLinkedWorktrees PASSES at the head. I then removed the byCommonDir sharing in attach (cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go), replacing the lookup with an unconditional fresh allocation:\n  current := \u0026room{workspace: workspace}\n  s.byCommonDir[workspace.CommonDir] = current\nUnder that mutation the new test FAILS at main_test.go:982, \"one adapter allocated separate room and projection caches for linked checkouts\". Running the whole cmd/gitseq-mcp package under the same mutation, that test is the ONLY failure. This confirms both halves of my prior verdict 4f50947f: the invariant really was uncovered, and this one test is now its sole cover. Mutation reverted; tree verified clean at c80862a0 before gates.\n\nThe test drives ONE mcpServer across a main checkout and a real linked worktree (git worktree add), asserts via app.ResolveGitDirs that the two checkouts have DISTINCT GitDir and identical CommonDir, then asserts pointer identity mainRoom == linkedRoom, that byCommonDir holds exactly one entry mapping the common dir to that room, and that both checkout paths in byPath resolve to it. That is the room/projection cache identity the request named. The sibling TestAdapterStartsFromMainAndLinkedWorktreeInOneWorkroom constructs TWO adapters, which is precisely why it never exercised this sharing.\n\nHERMETICITY: t.TempDir() root, git init inside it, no fixed ports, no network, no running resident required, no dependence on the repository's own worktrees.\n\nARCHITECTURE: affected layer is Surface, cmd/gitseq-mcp (docs/reference/architecture.md section 7: adapts MCP calls to Workroom and nexus operations). The head is test-only, so the surface contract - MCP protocol version, tool schemas, connector behavior - is unchanged. The test reads unexported fields from inside package main; it adds no exported surface. The contract is preserved, so no architecture page update is due at this head.\n\nSECURITY: the invariant under test is itself an authority-boundary property, and the code comment at the attach site records the risk it guards - resolving \"the default\" or \"the only attachment\" after the fact could hand back another repository's addressed inbox and leased focus. This test pins the sharing direction (two checkouts of ONE repository share one attachment), while the sibling test pins the separation direction (distinct repositories stay distinct). The actor authorization in attach, workspace.Actor(s.actor) before any caching, is unchanged. No untrusted input, secret, signature, bound, or failure mode is introduced; the fixture is self-constructed. No security defect found.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut without weakening the conditions of satisfaction. app.ResolveGitDirs is not redundant with reading the room, because the sharing collapses both checkouts into one room and the linked GitDir is unrecoverable afterwards; resolving it up front is the only way to prove the two paths are genuinely distinct checkouts. The byCommonDir and byPath assertions go slightly beyond the room identity check, but they pin the mechanism the request named and would catch sharing that arrived by an unintended route. The fixture prologue duplicates the sibling test, but inline fixtures are this file's established style - six inline git-init fixtures, and the one helper initRepository does not cover linked worktrees - so extracting a helper for this test alone would single it out against the file's own convention and is not a condition of satisfaction.\n\nGATES at the exact head, verbatim tail of go build ./... \u0026\u0026 go vet ./... \u0026\u0026 go test ./... -count=1:\nBUILD OK\nVET OK\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq 1.121s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/cmd/gitseq-github 1.962s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/cmd/gitseq-mcp 60.229s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/cmd/gitseq-perf 3.598s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/cmd/gs 191.743s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/app 57.483s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/connector/github 10.253s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/custody 12.427s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/docset 131.110s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/domain 20.940s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/gitstore 26.596s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/intent 18.484s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/kernel 154.930s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/nexus 9.260s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/observe 17.449s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/perflane 14.433s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/perfscenario 15.484s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/residentclient 21.474s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/service 46.348s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/statusview 22.280s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/telemetry 20.984s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/wireparity 19.206s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/workroom 19.124s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/spike/cmd/gitseq-report 18.911s\nok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/spike/cmd/gitseq-spike 21.35